s such as  currency signs  so until this point uh basically i tried  to use a tesseract ocr engine on the  data that i collected  um  and i found out that it's not the right  tool for me however i didn't give up  on the possibility of using an ocr  engine  on my data and i started searching  online for other ocr engines  luckily i came across  a post by jan lee kuhn who is uh one of  the godfathers of deep learning and head  of  a aiat meta and jan le kun recommends in  his post um  an ocr engine called easy ocr um and  this is a  tool that is based on pytorch and uses  um  modern deep learning algorithms and so i  decided that if jan le [ __ ] recommends  something i should definitely try it  um so let's see how easy ocr works  so  first it gets an input image then it  applies pre-processing on it  next stage uh it detects  text out of uh  of of the image using a model called  craft please remember in the name we'll  return to it later  and after that it performs um  a text recognition and so it turns um  the  characters or the text detected earlier  into a sequence of characters and in the  last end uh in last part it uh  basically decodes their sequence of  characters into a meaningful text  so  each part of this process is actually um  can be  replaced by a different model it's a  very modular  system  and it's  let's see how it works  on the examples that we saw earlier so  here we see that easy ocr does a better  job on those examples  but let's see  two more examples  here we see that easy ocr makes mistakes  on  some of the characters  and such as  the s sign it replaces it with the  dollar sign or  it  doesn't recognize well the percentage  sign  and  so let's see  what  i found out about easy ocr so the first  thing is that easy ocr is um  meant for detecting  text in the wild so um  tesseract works best is meant for  detecting text on documents while easy  ocr detects text in the world which  means that it recognizes text in  natural scenes such as street signs for  example  it's based on pie torch which i  personally think is a pro  but not everyone would agree with me  it's a really modular system each uh  part of it can be replaced by a  different model  and  as its name suggests it's easy to use  and there is a really nice python  package for that  the concept i saw is that first  it  doesn't work that well on black and  white images  which  were a tesseract performs well  and  it makes mistakes on special characters  such as the percentage signs and  currency signs  so  until this point basically i tried  two ocr engines in order to solve  my problem of  classifying text overlaid on top of an  image into a readable or not readable  text and  i came to the conclusion  that  neither of these  systems works for me  and  i  i try to think how i can combine them or  maybe trying a different ocr engine but  i came to the conclusion that um  the the those system might be too  complex and it might be hard to maintain  and debug  and so  at this time stage i decided to  recalculate my route  and to try a different perspective  so  basically the perspective i tried so far  it was trying to  use a poc  code and make it work  and  until this point i learned a lot of  things and i decided to try my own a  build my own system my own code based on  the things that i learned so far  and so  as part of the this  perspective change i decided to use  three basic principles um  in my  development stage and the first one is  splitting  big problems into  smaller problems the second one is using  only the relevant existing parts and not  using big system  just  because it's there  and the third one is finding the right  balance in my data set  to make  the best out of my model  and let's go out  over each one of those principles  so the first principle is really simple  is splitting a big problem into smaller  problems  um so we have here a text  it's written here and feel good  and by looking at this text we can see  that um the text is not very clear but  it's hard to quantify  however if we go over each one of those  characters for example  the e on the right and the o on the left  we can see easily that the e is readable  and the o is not  and so if we go over each one of those  characters we can calculate the  the ratio of the bad characters and the  ones that are not clear and not readable  out of the  entire text  and  according to a predefined threshold for  example if we decide that um  only  texts that have  a 0.8 um  good  contrast ratio out of everything and  then the text is a is readable in this  case this text won't pass and it would  fail our  our  condition  so  the next principle is using only the  relevant existing parts for  my problem until this point i used ocr  engines as black boxes  and  when they failed  for example when tesseract returned  gibberish it was really hard for me to  debug and to understand what is going on  um  and instead of just using a big black  box i decided to use only a small part  out of um  out of the systems that i tried i  decided to use the craft model which is  meant for um  detecting text  and so i  used craft which is  a model that basically  calculates for each  pixel in the image the probability of  this pixel to be in a center of  of a character  and based on this probability  we can build a heat map where the high  probability and  would be the  area where  that are red and in the areas that we  have lower probability the colors would  be blue  and so we can uh  extract out of  the image uh the bounding boxes of uh of  the characters and and we can have uh  small  images of the characters in the text  and after that the next stage is  basically classifying all those  of those characters into  readable or not readable  characters  and this leads me to the third principle  which is finding the right balance in  the data set  basically i model my  this  problem of  classifying characters into readable or  not readable characters as a binary  classification problem  however it's not actually a binary as we  have some scale  where in one hand we have characters  that are very  clear and readable and on the other hand  we have characters that  are not  um and this scale is actually very  subjective and  every person has their own scale and  people with  low vision might have a different scale  at all  so  another thing that affects  the problem when trying to label this  data  is that uh we sometimes have  an effect that is called anti-aliasing  so what is anti-aliasing is basically a  method of rendering um rendering on a  screen and when we talk about  fonts and characters basically it does  some smoothing effects on the characters  and however when the characters and the  fonts are  very thin it means that the  color of the text blends into the  background and so it makes  things  look  much  worse than  they are actually  and  and so uh when i tried  um using the label data that i had  that was manually labeled  i had a bunch of  subjective labels basically and um  and the model that i trained on this  data didn't perform that well  um and  so i decided to enrich the data set that  i had with a label that were objective  that were labeled based on the standard  that defines exactly what is a  readable  character and what is not and  i performed a series of experiments  where i trialled different balances  between those two groups until i had a  model that performed well and  and did well on  the entire set  so how does the entire solution work  so first uh it gets an input image and a  text  and then it runs craft in order to  extract characters  uh from the text  each of those characters is  labeled um and classified into a  readable or not readable character and  after that um  the bad contrast ratio  character  a is  computed  and in the end based on the threshold we  classify the entire text  and we assess if it's accessible or not  so until this point  i explained to you how i try to use  ocr 